<title>USA: Stormy weather temporarily stops Minnesota harvest.</title>
<headline>Stormy weather temporarily stops Minnesota harvest.</headline>
<dateline>ST PAUL, Minn. 1996-11-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Early in the week, stormy conditions temporarily delayed harvest activity in Minnesota, according to the state's Agricultural Statistics Service in its latest crop report.</p>
<p>Corn borer injury became more apparent as heavy winds produced locally severe stalk breakage and ear damage, the report said.</p>
<p>As of Sunday, 78 percent of corn for grain had been harvested, which was four points behind last year's pace of 82 percent and 12 points ahead of the five-year average of 66 percent.</p>
<p>Moisture content of corn being harvested averaged 21 percent, compared with 18 percent in 1995 and the five-year average of 22 percent.</p>
<p>The sunflower harvest is now 95 percent complete, versus 88 percent a year ago and 88 percent on average.</p>
<p>Topsoil moisture was rated 22 percent surplus, 73 percent adequate and 5 percent short.</p>
<p>--Chicago Newsdesk 312-408-8720--</p>
